1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,008
A robot watches a video.

2
00:00:02,008 --> 00:00:07,426
It tries to copy a human. But can it learn
what the camera never sees?

3
00:00:07,606 --> 00:00:09,639
A single camera records flat images.

4
00:00:09,639 --> 00:00:13,648
A robot needs the motion of real 3D parts,
even when a hand hides them.

5
00:00:13,828 --> 00:00:16,831
UC Berkeley researchers built
Robot See Robot Do,

6
00:00:16,831 --> 00:00:20,140
presented at the Conference on Robot
Learning in 2024.

7
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,087
First, they scan the object.

8
00:00:22,087 --> 00:00:27,073
From one demonstration video, the model
estimates how the parts move over time.

9
00:00:27,253 --> 00:00:29,891
That reconstructed motion
guides a robot planner.

10
00:00:29,891 --> 00:00:33,768
The robot can attempt the movement,
even from a different starting pose.

11
00:00:33,948 --> 00:00:38,167
But here's the catch: a bad scan, hidden
parts, or the wrong camera angle

12
00:00:38,167 --> 00:00:41,982
can break the reconstruction.
One video is not a universal lesson.

13
00:00:42,162 --> 00:00:45,589
Their experiments include hinged objects
and a moving toy.

14
00:00:45,589 --> 00:00:47,775
So what exactly is the robot copying?

15
00:00:47,955 --> 00:00:50,826
Not the human hand,
but the object's movement.

16
00:00:50,826 --> 00:00:55,631
Save this video if you found it useful,
and subscribe for the next discovery.
